  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nMountain rain showers persist through tonight slowly \nchanging to snow showers in the higher elevations.\n\nFront is surging east and cold advection taking place in the\nmountains. Still should be til later this evening into the\novernight for any rain to change to snow showers in the\nmountains. Brisk northwest winds will gust at times to 20-30\nmph into tonight and Friday, highest in the mountains, with a\nfew gusts over 35 mph. Look for clearing skies this evening in\nthe Piedmont while the mountains linger in the clouds through\nSaturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nUpslope snow event through Saturday for the western mountains, \nmuch colder air through the weekend before a warmup by late next\nweek. \n\nAs subsequent shortwave troughs swing through the area into \nFriday, upslope snow will continue across the western mountains \nof NC/VA/WV. Due to the ground being warm, initial snow will \nmostly melt, but as the cold air settles in, light accumulations\nwill occur. Flurries will be possible anywhere along/west of \nI-81 on Friday, and generally up to 0.5\" is possible for the \nwestern mountains outside of the higher elevations, where 1-3\" \nis possible, especially on elevated or grassy surfaces. It is \npossible a few areas such as Western Greenbrier County or Mt. \nRogers could exceed 3\", but confidence in that occurring is low.\nSnow showers continue for the western mountains through midday \nSaturday before tapering off as the upper-trough begins to lift \noff to the northeast. \n\nThe cold air will be the bigger story for most, as it continues\nto pour into the area from Canada, as a large upper trough sits\noverhead. This will keep highs around 5-10 degrees below normal\nfor the Piedmont due to clear skies, while heavier cloud cover \nfrom the northwest flow will keep highs 15-20 degrees below \nnormal for locations west of the Blue Ridge. Highs each day\nthrough the weekend will be in the low 50s for the Piedmont, \nand in the 30s to low 40s for the mountains. Overnight lows \nfall into the 20s area-wide Saturday and Sunday nights. \n\nWinds will also remain elevated through the weekend, gusting to\n20-25 mph Friday and Saturday, lowering to around 20 mph Sunday\nand becoming light by Monday as high pressure builds back into \nthe area. Due to these winds along with the cold temperatures, \nwind chills will be in the teens/20s Saturday and Sunday \nmornings. While this is not a dangerous level of cold, it will \nbe quite the shock to the system after the mild last week or so.\nBy early next week, a gradual warming trend occurs, with highs \nin the 50s by Tuesday and in the 60s by Thursday with no rain \nexpected all week.",
